For $k gt.eq 1$, put $S_k = V \( cal(I)_k \)$. The inclusions $cal(I)_1^k subset cal(I)_k subset
  cal(I)_1$ show that $sqrt(cal(I)_k) = cal(I)_1$, so these thickenings have the common underlying floor.
  Their conormal sequence is

$ 0 arrow.r cal(E)_k arrow.r cal(O)_(S_(k + 1)) arrow.r cal(O)_(S_k) arrow.r 0 . $

Use the underlying continuous map $\| phi.alt \| : \| S \| arrow.r \| W \|$ to put $cal(B)_k = \| phi.alt \|_(*)
  cal(O)_(S_k)$. Then $cal(B)_1 = cal(O)_W$, and (4.2) gives epimorphisms of sheaves of rings on $\| W \|$

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
 $ cal(B)_(k + 1) arrow.r.twohead cal(B)_k wide \( k gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:thickening-ring-surjection>]

This is the immediate cohomological assertion used in #cite(label("DasHacon2024OnMMP")) Claim 5.9.
  Exactness is stalkwise, and no splitting of the augmentation $cal(B)_k arrow.r cal(O)_W$ is assumed.
  The next construction realizes these ringed spaces analytically and constructs the corresponding maps from $S_k$.

=== 4.1 Analytic realization of the floor thickenings <subsec:analytic-thickenings>

Retain the divisorial ideals $cal(I)_k$, the thickenings $S_k$, the map $phi.alt : S arrow.r
  W$ from Proposition 7, and the sheaves $cal(B)_k$ in (4.11).
  We construct their analytic structure from the already analytic source thickenings.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (Analytic base thickenings).  #emph[For every $k gt.eq 1$, put
$ W_k = \( \| W \| \, cal(B)_k \) . $

 Then $W_k$ is a complex space with canonical reduction $W$,
   and the canonical sheaf evaluation defines a proper surjective holomorphic map $phi.alt_k : S_k arrow.r W_k$ with
$ \( phi.alt_k \)_(*) cal(O)_(S_k) = cal(O)_(W_k) . $

 Its reduction is $phi.alt$. The quotients $cal(B)_(k + 1) arrow.r.twohead
   cal(B)_k$ define closed analytic embeddings $W_k arrow.r.hook W_(k + 1)$.
   They commute with the embeddings $S_k arrow.r.hook S_(k + 1)$ and the maps $phi.alt_k$.]
] <lem:analytic-thickenings>

#proof[
  #emph[Canonical rings and their successive kernels.]
  All direct images defining $cal(B)_k$ first use the continuous map $\| phi.alt \| : \| S \| arrow.r \| W \|$.
    The equality $cal(B)_1 = cal(O)_W$ is the canonical equality for the holomorphic map $phi.alt$.
    Apply derived direct image for abelian sheaves to the conormal sequence.
    Its derived image on an $cal(O)_S$-module is the underlying sheaf of the analytic higher direct image.
    To see this formally, an injective $cal(O)_S$-module is flasque:
    for open sets $V subset U$, the extension-by-zero modules satisfy $j_(V !) cal(O)_V arrow.r.hook j_(U !)
    cal(O)_U$. Adjunction identifies $"Hom"_(cal(O)_S) \( j_(U !) cal(O)_U \, J \)$ with $Gamma \( U \, J \)$,
    so injectivity of $J$ makes the restriction of sections surjective.
    Flasque abelian sheaves are acyclic for continuous direct image.
    An injective module resolution therefore computes both the analytic module higher images and their underlying
    abelian-sheaf higher images. Thus (4.2) gives exact sequences
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
   $ 0 arrow.r cal(F)_k := phi.alt_(*) cal(E)_k arrow.r cal(B)_(k + 1) arrow.r cal(B)_k arrow.r
     0 wide \( k gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:analytic-thickening-sequence>]
  The displayed quotients are ring maps; exactness here concerns their underlying abelian sheaves.
    Proper direct-image coherence for the already holomorphic $phi.alt$
    #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesVIII")) Theorem 1.1, p. 15-01 makes $cal(F)_k$ a coherent $cal(O)_W$-module.
  In $cal(O)_(S_(k + 1))$, the ideal $cal(E)_k$ is square-zero and is annihilated by $cal(I)_1$:
    use $cal(I)_k^2 subset cal(I)_(2 k) subset cal(I)_(k + 1)$ and $cal(I)_1 cal(I)_k subset cal(I)_(k + 1)$.
    Consequently the action of $cal(B)_(k + 1)$ on the ideal $cal(F)_k$ factors through $cal(B)_1 = cal(O)_W$.
    This is the $cal(O)_W$-module structure on the square-zero ideal used in the following construction.
  The composites of (4.12) are epimorphisms $cal(B)_k arrow.r cal(O)_W$. Their kernels are
  $ cal(N)_k = \| phi.alt \|_(*) \( cal(I)_1 \/ cal(I)_k \) \, wide cal(N)_k^k = 0 \, $
  by left exactness and $cal(I)_1^k subset cal(I)_k$. Since $W$ is reduced, $cal(N)_k$ is exactly the nilradical.
    Each stalk of $cal(B)_k$ is local: an element lifting a unit in $cal(O)_(W \, w)$ has an inverse by a finite
    geometric series in the nilpotent kernel, whereas an element mapping to the maximal ideal is not a unit.
    Its residue field is $bb(C)$. Thus $W_k$ is already a locally ringed space,
    and it remains to construct local analytic presentations for it.
  #emph[A local analytic presentation from source functions.]
  Fix $k$ and $w in W$. Choose a local closed analytic embedding of a neighborhood of $w$ into an open polydisc.
    Lift the finitely many germs of its coordinate restrictions through $cal(B)_k arrow.r cal(O)_W$,
    represent the lifts on a common neighborhood, and shrink the polydisc.
    This gives a closed embedding $i : U arrow.r.hook D subset bb(C)^d$ and sections $b_1 \, dots.h \,
    b_d in cal(B)_k \( U \)$ reducing to the coordinate restrictions.
    Put $Z = S_(k \, U)$, the open subspace of $S_k$ with underlying set $phi.alt^(- 1) \( U \)$.
    By definition, $cal(B)_k \( U \) = H^0 \( Z \, cal(O)_Z \)$.
  For a possibly nonreduced complex space, a tuple of holomorphic functions defines a holomorphic map to affine
    space, functorially on structure sheaves #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesIII")) Theorem 1.1, p. 10-02.
    Apply this theorem to the $b_j$. Their point values lie in $D$, so the map factors as
  $ g : Z arrow.r D . $
  The same tuple theorem identifies its actual analytic restriction to the reduction with $i phi.alt$,
    because the reduced coordinate functions are those of $i phi.alt$. In particular $\| g \| = i \| phi.alt \|$.
    The restriction $phi.alt^(- 1) \( U \) arrow.r U$ is proper and $i$ is closed, so $g$ is proper;
    nilpotents do not change properness of the underlying continuous map.
  The holomorphic map $g$ makes $cal(A) = g_(*) cal(O)_Z$ a genuine $cal(O)_D$-algebra.
    Its underlying module is coherent by the proper direct-image theorem
    #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesVIII")) Theorem 1.1.
    Directly from the underlying maps, as sheaves of $bb(C)$-algebras,
  $ cal(A) = i_(*) \( cal(B)_k \|_U \) . $
  Reduction gives an epimorphism $cal(A) arrow.r i_(*) cal(O)_U$.
    It is $cal(O)_D$-linear because the actual analytic restriction $g \|_S =
    i phi.alt$ identifies the reduced action of every ambient holomorphic function with its restriction to $U$.
    The target is the coherent quotient of $cal(O)_D$ by the ideal of $U$.
    Hence its kernel $cal(N)$ is a coherent $cal(O)_D$-module. It is also a nilpotent ideal.
    Shrink $D$ once more and choose sections $n_1 \, dots.h \, n_r$ generating $cal(N)$ as a module.
    They are actual sections of $cal(O)_Z$ whose reductions vanish.
    Since $cal(O)_D arrow.r i_(*) cal(O)_U$ is an epimorphism,
    subtraction of an ambient lift of a reduction gives the stalkwise module equality
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
   $ cal(A) = cal(O)_D dot.op 1 + sum_(nu = 1)^r cal(O)_D dot.op n_nu . $ <eq:analytic-module-generation>]
  No splitting of the reduction map is used here.
  Apply the tuple theorem again to the $b_j$ and $n_nu$. It gives
  $ H = \( g \, n_1 \, dots.h \, n_r \) : Z arrow.r P = D times bb(C)^r . $
  If $j : U arrow.r.hook P$ is $u mapsto \( i \( u \) \, 0 \)$, then $\| H \| = j \| phi.alt \|$,
    since the $n_nu$ are nilpotent. Thus $H$ is proper. The sheaf $cal(A)' =
    H_(*) cal(O)_Z$ is a coherent $cal(O)_P$-module and the unit is an algebra map
  $ theta : cal(O)_P arrow.r cal(A)' . $
  As sheaves of rings $cal(A)' = j_(*) \( cal(B)_k \|_U \)$, so its stalk at $j \( u \)$ is $cal(B)_(k \, u) =
    cal(A)_(i \( u \))$, and its stalk outside $j \( U \)$ is zero.
    The stalk identification uses the cofinality of restrictions of ambient neighborhoods among neighborhoods in
    the closed subspace $U$.
  The map $theta$ is surjective on every stalk. At $j \( u \)$, any element of the target has, by (4.13),
    the form $a_0 + sum a_nu n_nu$ with $a_nu in cal(O)_(D \, i \( u \))$.
    The projection of $H$ to $D$ is $g$, and the extra coordinate $t_nu$ pulls back to $n_nu$.
    The element is therefore the image of the convergent germ
  $ a_0 \( z \) + sum_(nu = 1)^r a_nu \( z \) t_nu in cal(O)_(P \, j \( u \)) . $
  At all other stalks the target is zero. The kernel $cal(Q) =
    ker theta$ is a coherent analytic ideal #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesII")) pp. 9-07–9-08.
    It defines a closed complex subspace $Y = V \( cal(Q) \) subset
    P$ by #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesII")) Definition 2.2 and adjacent construction, p. 9-10.
    Its support is exactly $j \( U \)$, since the target stalk there surjects onto the nonzero ring
    $cal(O)_(U \, u)$. Under the homeomorphism with $U$, its structure sheaf is $cal(B)_k \|_U$,
    and its reduction is $U$.
  Every germ of $cal(Q)$ pulls back to zero by the definition of $theta$.
    The closed-subspace factorization criterion #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesII")) pp. 9-04–9-05
    therefore factors $H$ holomorphically through $Y$. Its sheaf map, under the identified structure sheaf,
    is the canonical evaluation
  $ \| phi.alt \|^(- 1) \( cal(B)_k \|_U \) arrow.r cal(O)_Z . $
  Indeed every germ of $cal(B)_k \|_U$ lifts through the surjective $theta$ to an ambient germ,
    whose pullback is precisely its value as a section on the inverse image.
    This proves the assertion for every germ, not only the chosen coordinates.
  #emph[Gluing and closed transition maps.]
  The ringed space $\( \| W \| \, cal(B)_k \)$ was fixed before any of the local choices.
    The presentations just constructed make each of its restrictions an analytic space by the local definition and
    adjacent construction in #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesII")) Definitions 2.1–2.2 and adjacent prose.
    On overlaps the identifications are the identity on the same sheaf of $bb(C)$-algebras,
    and hence are analytic isomorphisms satisfying the cocycle condition
    #cite(label("Grothendieck1961TechniquesII")) Definition 2.4 and Proposition 2.5.
    This proves that $W_k$ is a complex space. It retains the Hausdorff topology of $W$,
    and its reduction is canonically $W$. Write $epsilon_k : \| phi.alt \|^(- 1) cal(B)_k arrow.r
    cal(O)_(S_k)$ for the global sheaf evaluation. It is locally the holomorphic factorization above,
    so it defines $phi.alt_k$. Its reduction is $phi.alt$, and its underlying map is $\| phi.alt \|$;
    thus it is proper and surjective. The direct-image equality is the definition of its target structure sheaf,
    with the canonical unit as the identification.
  For the closed transition, use the construction with $H$ at level $k +
    1$ and restrict its coordinate functions to the closed subspace $S_(k \, U)$.
    They give another proper holomorphic map to the same $P$, with underlying map $j \| phi.alt \|$.
    Its unit is the composite
  $ cal(O)_P arrow.r.twohead j_(*) \( cal(B)_(k + 1) \|_U \) arrow.r.twohead j_(*) \( cal(B)_k \|_U \) . $
  The second quotient stays surjective under the closed embedding $j$, as seen on its stalks.
    The last sheaf with this ambient action is coherent by proper direct image.
    The two units therefore have nested coherent ideal kernels,
    so their analytic subspaces give the required closed inclusion.
    These local inclusions glue because their ring maps are the fixed quotients. Naturality of the evaluation maps gives
  $ epsilon_k compose \|
    phi.alt \|^(- 1) \( cal(B)_(k + 1) arrow.r cal(B)_k \) = \( cal(O)_(S_(k + 1)) arrow.r cal(O)_(S_k) \) compose
    epsilon_(k + 1) \, $
  which proves commutativity with the source inclusions. No retraction $W_k arrow.r W$, Cartesian square,
    or realization of the infinite tower as one formal completion is asserted or needed. ◻
]

=== 4.2 Extension of a prime-floor contraction <subsec:prime-floor-extension>

#thm([Proposition 4.3.])[
  (Extension from a prime floor).  #emph[Under the hypotheses of Proposition 7,
    there are a normal compact analytic space $Z$, a closed embedding $W arrow.r.hook Z$,
    and a proper bimeromorphic morphism $F : X arrow.r Z$ such that $F \|_S$ is the given map $phi.alt$ followed
    by that embedding and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
 $ X \\ S tilde.eq Z \\ W \, wide F^(- 1) \( \| W \| \) = \| S \| \,
   wide \| F^(- 1) \( w \) \| = \| phi.alt^(- 1) \( w \) \| quad \( w in W \) . $ <eq:extension-fibers>]

 The natural map $cal(O)_Z arrow.r F_(*) cal(O)_X$ is an isomorphism, and the fibers are connected.
   For a sufficiently divisible global index $ell$ of $S$, the actual line $cal(O)_X \( - ell S \)$ is $F$-ample;
   in particular $F$ is projective. More generally an actual rational line on $X$ whose restriction to $S$ is
   $phi.alt$-ample is $F$-ample. The target is in Fujiki’s class $cal(C)$.]
] <prop:prime-floor-extension>

#proof[
  Choose a positive global index $ell$ such that $ell S$ is Cartier and $cal(O)_S \( - ell S \)$ is $phi.alt$-ample. Put
  $ A = S_ell = ell S \, wide A' = W_ell \, wide f = phi.alt_ell : A arrow.r A' . $
  Here $A$ is the full effective Cartier divisor, including its nilpotents;
    its local equation on normal $X$ is a nonzerodivisor. Lemma 8 makes $f$ a proper surjective holomorphic map of
    complex spaces and gives the natural equality $f_(*) cal(O)_A = cal(O)_(A')$.
  Cartier multiplication gives $cal(I)_ell cal(I)_j = cal(I)_(j + ell)$.
    Hence, for every integer $n > 0$, on the full space $A$ one has
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.15)")
   $ cal(O)_A \( - n A \) = cal(I)_(n ell) ⊗ cal(O)_A = cal(I)_(n ell) \/ cal(I)_(\( n + 1 \) ell) .
     $ <eq:cartier-negative-layer>]
  Its finite filtration by $cal(I)_j \/ cal(I)_(\( n + 1 \) ell)$, for $n ell lt.eq j lt.eq \( n + 1 \) ell$,
    has successive quotients $cal(E)_j$ for $n ell lt.eq j < \( n + 1 \) ell$. All indices are at least one.
    These filtration terms are $cal(O)_A$-modules, because $cal(I)_ell cal(I)_j subset cal(I)_(\( n + 1 \) ell)$.
  Let $a : S arrow.r.hook A$ and $i : W arrow.r.hook A'$ be the closed reduction embeddings.
    The compatibility in Lemma 8 gives $f a = i phi.alt$. Pushforward by a closed embedding is exact,
    so the composition identity for derived direct images of abelian sheaves gives
  $ R^q f_(*) \( a_(*) cal(E)_j \) = i_(*) R^q phi.alt_(*) cal(E)_j = 0 quad \( q > 0 \) . $
  The long exact sequences of the finite filtration in (4.15) therefore give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.16)")
   $ R^q f_(*) cal(O)_A \( - n A \) = 0 wide \( q > 0 \, med n > 0 \) . $ <eq:cartier-negative-vanishing>]
  This uses no vanishing for $cal(E)_0$ and keeps the full Cartier thickening throughout.
  The actual line $cal(O)_A \( - A \)$ restricts to $cal(O)_S \( - ell S \)$.
    The underlying map of $f$ is $phi.alt$, so the reductions of their fibers, as closed subspaces of $X$,
    coincide: a reduced closed analytic subspace is determined by its support.
    Thus $cal(O)_A \( - A \)$ is ample on the reduction of every $f$-fiber.
    For a line on a compact nonreduced complex space, ampleness on the reduction implies ampleness on the whole
    space. Indeed the positive metric from #cite(label("Fujino2026Fujiki")) Corollary 1.12 has local strictly
    plurisubharmonic weights in common ambient embeddings, by its Lemma 2.4.
    The same weights define a metric on the original line, since the pointwise absolute values of transition units
    are unchanged by nilpotents. The converse positive-metric criterion gives ampleness on the full space.
    The proper fiberwise criterion #cite(label("Fujino2026Fujiki")) Definition 3.1 and Remark 3.2 now makes
    $cal(O)_A \( - A \)$ $f$-ample.
  We apply the analytic blowdown criterion stated in #cite(label("DasHacon2024OnMMP")) Theorem 4.2, p. 14,
    attributed there to #cite(label("Fujiki1975")) Theorem 2, p. 495.
    For a reduced complex space, an effective Cartier divisor $A$ with its full possibly nonreduced structure,
    and a proper surjective holomorphic map $f : A arrow.r A'$,
    this criterion assumes $f$-ampleness of $cal(O)_A \( - A \)$ and
  $ R^1 f_(*) cal(O)_A \( - n A \) = 0 wide upright("for every ") n > 0 . $
  It supplies a blowing down: a complex target $Z$ containing $A'$ as an embedded subspace,
    a proper surjective holomorphic map $F : X arrow.r Z$ whose restriction to $A$ is $f$,
    and an analytic isomorphism of the complements. In its universal form it also supplies the natural equality of
    sheaves of rings
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.17)")
   $ F_(*) cal(S)_(X \, A \, f) = cal(O)_Z . $ <eq:universal-blowdown-ring>]
  Here $cal(S)_(X \, A \, f)$ consists along $A$ of the germs in $cal(O)_X$ whose restrictions lie locally in
    $"im" \( f^(- 1) cal(O)_(A') arrow.r cal(O)_A \)$, and is $cal(O)_X$ away from $A$.
    This is a subsheaf of rings; no module coherence of this particular sheaf is used.
    The hypotheses of the stated criterion follow from (4.16) and the preceding ampleness argument.
    No flatness or reducedness of $A$ or $A'$ is required.
  The embedded $A'$ is closed because it is compact. Its reduction embeds the original $W$ as a closed subspace of
    $Z$. The compatible reductions give $F \|_S = phi.alt$ followed by this embedding.
    Removing a subspace depends only on its support, so the complement isomorphism is $X \\ S tilde.eq Z \\ W$.
    It follows that $F^(- 1) \( \| W \| \) = \| S \|$, and the prescribed restriction to $A$ gives the fiber-set
    equalities in (4.14). Outside $W$ the fibers are single reduced points.
    These assertions concern underlying fiber sets over $W$; they require no Cartesian square of the closed
    thickenings. The fibers are connected because the floor fibers are connected.
    The target is compact as the image of compact $X$.
  We next prove normality; it is not an extra conclusion assumed from the blowdown criterion.
    For every open $V subset Z$, a section $s in cal(O)_X \( F^(- 1) V \)$ restricts to a section on the open space
  $ A ∩ F^(- 1) V = f^(- 1) \( V ∩ A' \) . $
  The equality $f_(*) cal(O)_A = cal(O)_(A')$ makes this restriction the pullback of a unique section on $V ∩ A'$.
    Thus every germ of $s$ along $A$ satisfies the defining condition of $cal(S)_(X \, A \, f)$.
    The reverse inclusion follows from $cal(S)_(X \, A \, f) subset cal(O)_X$. Consequently
  $ F_(*) cal(S)_(X \, A \, f) = F_(*) cal(O)_X \, wide F_(*) cal(O)_X = cal(O)_Z $
  as sheaves of rings, the second equality by (4.17). This reasoning uses equality of the displayed open
    subspaces, not of any closed fiber products.
  The equality first makes $Z$ reduced. The complement is dense:
    the inverse image of a nonempty open of $Z$ is a nonempty open of $X$, and therefore meets $X \\ S$.
    The complement isomorphism thus makes $F$ bimeromorphic. Let $nu : Z^nu arrow.r
    Z$ be the finite analytic normalization. The finite normalization exists by #cite(label("Houzel1961")) Part B,
    Section 4, Corollaries 2–3. The dominant bimeromorphic map from normal $X$ lifts to $Z^nu$.
    Pullback along this lift gives inclusions, injective on the dense common complement,
  $ cal(O)_Z subset nu_(*) cal(O)_(Z^nu) subset F_(*) cal(O)_X = cal(O)_Z . $
  The normalization is consequently an isomorphism and $Z$ is normal.
    The natural direct-image equality also gives connected fibers by analytic Stein factorization.
  Finally consider the actual global line $cal(O)_X \( - ell S \)$.
    Its restriction is ample on every reduced $F$-fiber: this is clear off $W$,
    and over $W$ the reduction is the same embedded reduced space as that of the corresponding $phi.alt$-fiber.
    The nonreduced upgrade and the proper fiberwise criterion used above therefore make this one line $F$-ample.
    The same proof works for any actual ambient rational line with $phi.alt$-ample restriction after clearing its
    global index. In particular it works for the negative full adjoint in Proposition 7. Thus $F$ is projective.
    A projective resolution with smooth source of the compact Kähler $X$ is again compact Kähler,
    and its composite with $F$ is bimeromorphic. Hence $Z$ lies in Fujiki’s class $cal(C)$. ◻
]

The proposition supplies the ambient step for floor data satisfying its stated hypotheses,
  including the two actual ample-line conditions. In the threefold argument below it extends divisor-to-curve
  contractions. In Section 5 it extends a selected floor contraction of the supported fourfold program.
  When that floor contraction cuts out the selected ray, the exact fiber description keeps the same contracted
  curves, and the actual relatively ample lines make the ambient step projective.
  Its target Kähler class is then established in the supported-program argument.

=== 4.3 Descent of a dominating current <descent-of-a-dominating-current>

#thm([Lemma 4.4.])[
  (Descent of a dominating current).  #emph[Let $p : Y arrow.r
    Z$ be a proper bimeromorphic morphism between normal compact complex spaces, with $Y$ Kähler.
    Let $eta$ be a smooth real closed $\( 1 \, 1 \)$-form with local smooth potentials on $Z$.
    Suppose that a positive closed current $T$ with local potentials represents the local-potential Bott–Chern
    class $p^(*) \[ eta \]$, and that $T gt.eq kappa$ for a Kähler form $kappa$ on $Y$.
    For every smooth positive Hermitian form $g$ on $Z$, understood in local ambient embeddings,
    there are a constant $c > 0$ and a global quasi-plurisubharmonic function $u_Z$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.18)")
 $ eta + i partial accent(partial u, ‾)_Z gt.eq c g . $ <eq:dominating-current-descent>]

 This is a current in $\[ eta \]$ with local potentials, and it has the bigness property preceding
   #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Theorem 2.29.]
] <lem:dominating-current-descent>

#proof[
  We make the potential in the class equality explicit. Use the normal-space potential description of
    Boucksom–Guedj #cite(label("BoucksomGuedj2013")) Section 4.6.1,
    specifically Lemma 4.6.1 and the paragraph following Definition 4.6.2.
    The soft-sheaf descriptions of Bott–Chern cohomology by smooth functions and distributions first give a global
    real distribution $u_Y$ for which
  $ T = p^(*) eta + i partial accent(partial u, ‾)_Y . $
  Locally write $T = i partial accent(partial v, ‾)$ with $v$ plurisubharmonic and $p^(*) eta =
    i partial accent(partial h, ‾)$ with $h$ smooth. Then $u_Y + h - v$ is pluriharmonic as a distribution.
    By the cited lemma it is a smooth real part of a holomorphic germ.
    Thus $u_Y$ is locally represented by a quasi-plurisubharmonic function.
    These representatives agree as distributions on overlaps and hence,
    after taking their upper-semicontinuous representatives, agree pointwise.
    They give one global quasi-plurisubharmonic $u_Y$, which is locally integrable and locally bounded above.
  In local ambient embeddings, $p^(*) g$ is represented by a smooth semipositive form and $kappa$ by a smooth
    positive definite form. A finite relatively compact cover of $Y$ therefore gives a constant $C >
    0$ with $p^(*) g lt.eq C kappa$. Consequently
  $ p^(*) eta + i partial accent(partial u, ‾)_Y gt.eq C^(- 1) p^(*) g . $
  The map $p$ is a modification between normal spaces, and $u_Y$ has the local upper bound needed in the current
    descent argument. #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Corollary 2.32 and its proof,
    p. 18 gives a global quasi-plurisubharmonic $u_Z$ with (4.18) for $c = C^(- 1)$.
    It is an $L^1$ potential, and the formula keeps the descended current in the chosen Bott–Chern class.
    The local potentials of $eta$ give local potentials for that current. No Kähler form on $Z$ has been used. ◻
]

=== 4.4 Threefold contractions used in the boundary argument <subsec:threefold-contraction-bridge>

We prove Proposition 4 by applying the cited threefold MMP with two analytic constructions supplied here:
  Proposition 5 contracts the finite null loci, and Proposition 9 extends the prime-floor contractions.
  The projective relative MMP, the nonbig argument through a surface contraction,
  and the terminal canonical threefold program remain external inputs.
  We verify the hypotheses at each use of the two constructions, then supply the required target positivity;
  neither construction alone asserts that its target is Kähler.
  We first record the cone observation used in the big and floor cases.

For a normal compact space $Z$ in Fujiki’s class $cal(C)$, put $N^1 \( Z \) =
  H_(B C)^(1 \, 1) \( Z \)$ in the local-potential convention.
  Let $N_1 \( Z \)$ be the vector space of real closed currents of bidimension $\( 1 \, 1 \)$, modulo $T_1 equiv
  T_2$ when $T_1 \( eta \) = T_2 \( eta \)$ for every real closed $\( 1 \, 1 \)$-form $eta$ with local potentials;
  the pairing is evaluation. Following #cite(label("HoeringPeternell2016")) Definitions 3.6 and 3.8, pp. 8–9,
  let $"NA" \( Z \) = overline("NA") \( Z \) subset N_1 \( Z \)$ denote the closed cone generated by the numerical
  classes of positive closed currents. Thus both notations used below refer to this same closed cone.

#thm([Lemma 4.5.])[
  (Positivity on the current cone).  #emph[Let $Z$ be a normal compact Kähler analytic variety with rational
    singularities, and let $kappa$ be a Kähler class. In the finite-dimensional local-potential numerical spaces put
$ C = overline("NA") \( Z \) \, wide Sigma = { z in C : kappa dot.op z = 1 } . $

 Then $Sigma$ is compact. If a real local-potential $\( 1 \, 1 \)$-class $ell$ is strictly positive on $Sigma$,
   then $ell$ is Kähler.]
] <lem:na-slice>

#proof[
  By #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Lemma 2.3 and Proposition 2.4, the natural pairing is perfect,
    $"Nef" \( Z \) = C^(*)$, and the Kähler cone is open with closure the nef cone.
    In particular $kappa$ lies in the interior of $C^(*)$. A sequence in $Sigma$ with unbounded norm would,
    after division by its norm and passage to a subsequence, give a nonzero class $z in C$ with $kappa dot.op z =
    0$, contradicting that interior property. The slice is closed and hence compact.
  If it is nonempty, let $mu = min_Sigma ell > 0$. Homogeneity gives $ell - mu / 2 kappa in C^(*) =
    "Nef" \( Z \)$. A smooth local-potential nef approximation for this last class, bounded below by $-
    mu kappa \/ 4$, becomes a positive representative after adding $mu kappa \/ 2$. Thus $ell$ is Kähler.
    If $Sigma$ is empty, then $C = { 0 }$ by the interior property;
    the same conclusion follows because every class is nef and a positive small multiple of $kappa$ can be
    subtracted first. All cones here are the local-potential cones in the cited perfect pairing. ◻
]

The spaces used below have rational singularities: this is Lemma 2.31 of #cite(label("DasHacon2024")) for dlt
  pairs and its Remark 2.14 for klt varieties.

#proof[
  The proof of #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Theorem 5.5 first uses a projective relative small
    $bb(Q)$-factorialization and then #cite(label("DasHacon2024")) Theorem 1.7.
    The latter proof passes to a small strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial model and treats a nef nonbig class by its
    Theorem 5.5 and a nef big class by its Theorem 6.4. Here strong $bb(Q)$-factoriality means that every global
    coherent reflexive rank-one sheaf has an invertible reflexive power.
    It implies ordinary global $bb(Q)$-factoriality; it does not assert the analogous property for all analytic
    open subsets. The small models use the projective relative MMP of #cite(label("DasHacon2024"))
    Proposition 2.26 and Lemma 2.27, whose maps are already projective.
    Compactness of the final bases is retained when projective maps are composed,
    as required by #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Remark 2.11.
  ===== 4.4.1 The imported programs and target descent. <the-imported-programs-and-target-descent.>
  The nef nonbig branch follows the chain Theorem 5.5, Corollary 5.4,
    and Theorem 5.2 of #cite(label("DasHacon2024")). It uses Step 4 in the proof of
    #cite(label("HoeringPeternell2015")) Theorem 1.4, p. 242,
    which contracts a negative-definite collection of curves on a smooth compact Kähler surface and descends
    through a graph. We use that nonbig argument as an external input.
    The pseudo-effective cone lineage also uses the terminal canonical threefold program:
    Assumption 10.1 of #cite(label("DasOu2022")) invokes its nonvanishing Theorem 9.1,
    whose proof at p. 50 invokes #cite(label("HoeringPeternell2015")) Theorem 1.1 for a terminal $K$-MMP and Mori
    fiber space. We retain that terminal program as an external input.
    Together with the projective relative MMP just specified, these are the birational inputs used below.
  For an already constructed negative-ray contraction with ordinary $bb(Q)$-factorial compact Kähler dlt source
    and relatively ample negative adjoint, #cite(label("CampanaHoeringPeternell2016")) Proposition 3.1 gives
    rationality of the target and descent of the supporting class.
    We use the proof of its Corollary 3.1 for target positivity.
    The corollary assumes non-uniruledness to obtain a nef support; our ray already has an exposed nef support.
    Its remaining argument descends that class, proves positivity on the target current cone,
    and lifts target curves through the projective morphism. These steps apply to the data just stated.
    The proposition and corollary numbers here are those of the journal edition.
    We now verify the finite-null and prime-floor constructions to which this argument will be applied.
  ===== 4.4.2 Nef and big classes with a finite null locus. <nef-and-big-classes-with-a-finite-null-locus.>
  Three geometric situations use Proposition 5: a small negative ray, the entire null locus at a stopping model,
    and a small ray with pseudo-effective adjoint. The big threefold program uses strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial klt
    sources. The last situation can also occur for ordinary dlt data,
    whose underlying space is ordinary $bb(Q)$-factorial and klt, as checked below.
    In every case the relevant locus is the full top-intersection null locus of the proposition.
  For a small negative ray, the small branch of #cite(label("DasHacon2024")) proof of Theorem 4.16, p. 40 has,
    after its boundary perturbation, a strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial klt source and a nef big supporting class
    $a = \( K + Gamma' \) + kappa$ with $kappa$ Kähler. At this input #cite(label("DasHacon2024")) Lemma 4.5 says
    that an $a$-null surface is covered by $a$-null curves. All those curves lie in the exposed ray.
    Such a covering of a surface contradicts the smallness of that ray.
    Lemma 6 therefore makes $"Null" \( a \)$ a finite union of curves,
    and Proposition 5 constructs its contraction. This argument permits a non-pseudo-effective underlying adjoint.
  For the entire null locus at the final model $X_n$ of the program in #cite(label("DasHacon2024")) Claim 6.5 and
    proof of Theorem 6.4, pp. 50–51, the pair is strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial klt, its class $a_n$ is nef and big,
