=== 4.5 The actual floor split <subsec:actual-floor-split>

We verify the use of that specialization for the dlt floor in the fourfold supported program.
  Let $\( X \, S + B \)$ be one of its compact ordinary $bb(Q)$-factorial Kähler dlt pairs,
  with reduced floor $S$, coefficients of $B$ below one, and actual adjoint $A = K_X + S + B$.
  Fix a prime $T$ of $S$, and write

$ H = S - T \, wide A_0 = A - H = K_X + T + B . $

The finite global divisor $H$ is $bb(Q)$-Cartier by ordinary global factoriality,
  and $A_0$ is an actual rational line. On a dlt resolution,
  subtracting the effective pullback of $H$ lowers the exceptional crepant coefficients and removes the strict
  transforms of the other floor components. Only the strict transform of $T$ has coefficient one.
  Hence $\( X \, T + B \)$ is plt. Ordinary plt adjunction #cite(label("DasHacon2024OnMMP")) Lemma 5.3 gives a
  normal $T$, an effective rational boundary $B_0$ with $\( T \, B_0 \)$ klt,
  and the actual restriction $A_0 \|_T = K_T + B_0$.

Choose one positive integer $m$ clearing the indices of $A \, A_0 \, H$.
  The canonical section of $cal(O)_X \( m H \)$ restricts nontrivially to $T$ because $T$ is not a component of $H$. Set

$ B' = 1 / m "div" \( s_(m H) \|_T \) gt.eq 0 . $

It is an effective rational $bb(Q)$-Cartier divisor. Use compatible local meromorphic residue embeddings for the
  two adjunctions. Their ratio in codimension one on $T$ is multiplication by $s_(m H) \|_T$.
  Reflexive extension on normal $T$ then gives the divisor split and actual line identities

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.25)")
 $ B_T & = B_0 + B' \,\
cal(O)_T \( m \( K_T + B_0 \) \) & tilde.eq cal(O)_X \( m A_0 \) \|_T \,\
cal(O)_T \( m B' \) & tilde.eq cal(O)_X \( m H \) \|_T . $ <eq:actual-floor-boundary-split>]

where $A \|_T = K_T + B_T$ is the full dlt adjunction. This proves the split without assuming $T$ to be
  $bb(Q)$-factorial.

Suppose, as in the supported program, that $alpha_T = c_1 \( A \|_T \) + \[ omega_T \]$ is nef and $omega_T$ is
  Kähler. These properties hold by restricting the ambient smooth metric nef approximations and local strictly
  plurisubharmonic potentials. For a sufficiently small rational $epsilon > 0$, put

$ B_epsilon = B_0 + \( 1 - epsilon \) B' \, wide omega_epsilon = omega_T + epsilon theta_(B') \, $

where $theta_(B')$ is the normalized curvature of a smooth metric on a Cartier multiple of $B'$.
  The full adjunction is lc and the lower adjunction is klt.
  Affineness of discrepancies in this convex combination makes $\( T \, B_epsilon \)$ klt.
  On finitely many compact local embedding charts, the Hessian of $theta_(B')$ is bounded and that of $omega_T$ is
  uniformly positive. For the chosen small $epsilon$, $omega_epsilon$ is therefore Kähler and

$ alpha_T = c_1 \( K_T + B_epsilon \) + \[ omega_epsilon \] . $

If $dim T = 3$, Proposition 4 applies and gives a projective connected-fiber contraction $T arrow.r
  W$ to a normal compact Kähler target with $alpha_T$ pulled back from a Kähler class.
  This is the use of #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Corollary 5.6 in its Theorem 7.2.
  The restricted class $alpha_T$ is not assumed big, and its null face may have several rays.
  The contraction follows from the assembled argument in the preceding subsection,
  using Proposition 9 and the specified external results.

== 5 Reduction to a supported nef boundary <sec:reduction>

The positive-Iitaka-dimensional case is an application of a known Kähler abundance theorem.
  The purpose of this section is to prepare the remaining case for the two boundary arguments:
  from a nonzero divisor of a section in Iitaka dimension zero,
  we construct a nef dlt fourfold whose adjoint has a nonzero effective representative supported on exactly its
  reduced floor. We also construct the particular log resolution used to index and compare all strata of that floor.

#thm([Proposition 5.1.])[
  (Supported nef model).  #emph[Under the hypotheses of Theorem 1, suppose $kappa \( X \, D \) =
    0$ and the normalized rational divisor $M$ of a nonzero plurisection of $D$ is nonzero.
    Then there are a normal ordinary $bb(Q)$-factorial compact Kähler dlt fourfold $\( V \, B \)$,
    with effective rational boundary, and a nonzero effective rational $bb(Q)$-Cartier divisor $P$ such that
$ A = K_V + B upright(" is analytically nef") \, wide A tilde.op_(bb(Q)) P \, wide "Supp" P = "Supp" ⌊⌋ B \, wide
  kappa \( V \, A \) = 0 . $

 The pair has the projective resolution described in Lemma 17.]
] <prop:supported-model>

The resolution has globally smooth distinct SNC strict boundary and exceptional components,
  all exceptional crepant coefficients are below one, and it is generically an isomorphism on the image of every
  intersection component of distinct strict floor primes. These properties let the floor argument index its strata
  by actual boundary intersections. The proposition will follow from the construction below;
  the final step uses the original nef adjoint to show that $P$ cannot disappear.

We use the following analytic negativity theorem at several points.
  If $h : W arrow.r Z$ is a proper bimeromorphic morphism of normal irreducible analytic spaces and $E$ is a
  rational $bb(Q)$-Cartier divisor with $- E$ relatively nef, then

$ E gt.eq 0 quad arrow.l.r.double quad h_(*) E gt.eq 0 . $

This is #cite(label("Wang2021")) Lemma 1.3, after clearing an index.
  In particular, an exceptional relatively nef divisor is nonpositive.
  When $h$ is projective, nonnegative degrees on its contracted curves give the relative nefness used in this
  theorem; see #cite(label("Wang2021")) Appendix B. This relative curve test will not be used as a definition of
  nefness on a nonprojective compact Kähler space.

=== 5.1 Positive Iitaka dimension <positive-iitaka-dimension>

#thm([Proposition 5.2.])[
  #emph[Under the hypotheses of Theorem 1, if $kappa \( X \, D \) gt.eq 1$, then $D$ is semiample on $X$.]
] <prop:positive-kappa>

#proof[
  Das–Hacon–Păun’s dlt modification theorem #cite(label("DasHaconPaun2024")) Theorem 6.1 applies to a compact
    Kähler lc fourfold with effective rational boundary and $bb(Q)$-Cartier adjoint.
    It gives a projective bimeromorphic morphism
  $ g : \( X' \, Delta' \) arrow.r \( X \, Delta \) $
  with $X'$ compact Kähler and ordinary $bb(Q)$-factorial, the pair $\( X' \, Delta' \)$ dlt,
    and the adjoint crepant. The input is klt, so equality of discrepancies makes the output klt as well.
    The crepant equality is an equality of actual rational lines:
    pull a local pluriadjoint frame to a common resolution with smooth source as in (2.2),
    compare the crepant coefficients, and extend the identical meromorphic map across codimension two on the
    normal $X'$. Thus for a common index
  $ cal(O)_(X') \( m \( K_(X') + Delta' \) \) tilde.eq g^(*) cal(O)_X \( m D \) . $
  There is no possible undetected flat-line difference in this comparison.
  The pulled-back line is analytically nef by the metric criterion.
    Also, normality and projection formula identify all its sections with those of $cal(O)_X \( m D \)$.
    The meromorphic maps agree on the common dense open, so their Iitaka dimensions agree.
    Theorem 4.1 of Höring–Lazić–Lehn #cite(label("HoeringLazicLehn2025")) states that a nef adjoint of a compact
