=== 12.4 Invariant layers and the growth of sections <invariant-layers-and-the-growth-of-sections>

Finite lifting is now available in every degree. To obtain ambient sections,
  we pass to cyclic invariants and count the resulting finite quotients downstairs.
  Translation by the Cartier divisor $G$ will make the same finitely many coherent layers recur with increasing
  positive twists on $T$. Those twists supply the section growth and bound the higher-cohomology loss.

#proof[
  #emph[Completion of Theorem 2.] Define global divisorial subsheaves of meromorphic functions on the normal space
    $V$ by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12.16)")
   $ J_j = cal(O)_V (- ⌈j G \/ r⌉) quad \( j in bb(Z) \) . $ <eq:downstairs-ideals>]
  They agree in each root chart with $\( pi_(*) I^j \)^(mu_r)$.
    Indeed, at a prime over $S_i$, an invariant meromorphic function of downstairs order $v_i$ has order
    $\( r \/ d_i \) v_i$. Membership in $I^j$ is equivalent to
  $ v_i gt.eq ⌈j d_i \/ r⌉ . $
  At all other primes it is regular. The full invariant meromorphic algebra is the downstairs algebra,
    and normality extends the codimension-one test. Finite coherent pushforward and averaging by $mu_r$ are exact.
    This also proves coherence of the divisorial sheaves in (12.16) and of their quotients locally,
    and their valuation descriptions glue globally.
  Since $0 < d_i lt.eq r$, the sheaf $J_1 = cal(O)_V \( - S \)$ is the ideal of the reduced divisor $S$.
    The same coefficient inequality gives $J_1 J_j subset J_(j + 1)$ for every $j$. Hence
  $ cal(B)_j = J_j \/ J_(j + 1) $
  is a coherent $cal(O)_S$-module, and $cal(B)_0 = cal(O)_S$.
    Taking invariants of Proposition 30 preserves its epimorphisms:
    lift an invariant local section upstairs and average the lift.
    For $l < n$, define the sheaf of complex vector spaces
  $ cal(F)_(l \, n) = f_(*) \( J_l \/ J_n \) quad upright("on ") T \, wide F_j = f_(*) cal(B)_j . $
  Set also $cal(F)_(n \, n) = 0$. The quotients $J_l \/ J_n$ are considered on the underlying space of $S$,
    just as before. The invariant lifting surjections give exact sequences of complex vector space sheaves
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12.17)")
   $ 0 arrow.r cal(F)_(j + 1 \, n) arrow.r cal(F)_(j \, n) arrow.r F_j arrow.r
     0 quad \( j < n \) . $ <eq:downstairs-filtration>]
  In particular $cal(F)_(l \, n)$ has a finite filtration with the full quotients $F_j$, $l lt.eq j < n$.
    Each $F_j$ is a coherent $cal(O)_T$-module by properness of $f$;
    coherence as an $cal(O)_T$-module is not asserted for $cal(F)_(l \, n)$.
  Cartier translation by $G$ in (12.16), together with the actual line identity $cal(O)_V \( G \) =
    L$ defined by $s$, gives
  $ J_(j - r) = J_j ⊗ cal(O)_V \( G \) \, wide cal(B)_(j - r) = cal(B)_j ⊗ L \|_S . $
  Projection formula and (10.2) consequently give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12.18)")
   $ F_(j - r) = F_j ⊗ cal(O)_T \( 1 \) \, wide F_0 = f_(*) cal(O)_S eq.not 0 . $ <eq:periodic-layers>]
  This is an identity of the actual holomorphic lines and sheaves.
  For $N > 0$, each $- N r lt.eq j < 0$ is uniquely $j = l - t r$ with $0 lt.eq l < r$ and $1 lt.eq t lt.eq N$.
    By (12.18), $F_j = F_l \( t \)$. Analytic GAGA and Serre’s coherent finiteness and vanishing theorems
    #cite(label("Serre1956GAGA")) Section 3, no. 12, Theorems 1 and 3, #cite(label("Serre1955FAC")) Section 66,
    Theorems 1 and 2 imply, for every $i > 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12.19)")
   $ sum_(j = - N r)^(- 1) h^i \( T \, F_j \) lt.eq C_i := sum_(l = 0)^(r - 1) sum_(t gt.eq 1)
     h^i \( T \, F_l \( t \) \) < oo . $ <eq:higher-layer-bound>]
  These constants are independent of $N$, and they are zero for $i > dim T$.
    The long exact sequences of the finite filtration (12.17) give the same bounds for
    $h^i \( T \, cal(F)_(- N r \, 0) \)$, as well as finiteness and vanishing above that fixed dimension.
    Serre vanishing is being applied only to the coherent quotients $F_l \( t \)$.
  There is at least one section of $F_0 \( t \)$ for every $t gt.eq 1$.
    Choose a point of the nonempty $S$ and a section of $cal(O)_T \( t \)$ nonzero at its image;
    its pullback is nonzero there. Thus $sum_(j = - N r)^(- 1) h^0 \( T \, F_j \) gt.eq N$.
    Additivity of the finite Euler characteristics in (12.17),
    and the uniform higher bounds for both the quotients and $cal(F)$, give a constant $C$ independent of $N$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12.20)")
   $ h^0 \( T \, cal(F)_(- N r \, 0) \) gt.eq N - C . $ <eq:thickening-growth>]
